# Repos to "catch up" (rebase onto mainline) instead of pull.
#
# Each line is `name|mainline` (pipe-delimited, like machine-env-repos):
#   - name: a machine-env repo NAME (same names as in a *.machine-env-repos
#     manifest, or an auto-discovered $HOME/Yew/*-setup repo).
#   - mainline: the branch to rebase onto, as a remote-tracking ref like
#     `origin/main`. Optional — a bare `name` (no `|`) defaults the mainline to
#     `origin/<the repo's remote default branch>`.
# Comments (#) and blank lines are ignored.
#
# In catch-up mode gar measures how far the CURRENT branch is behind the mainline
# and offers to run `git fetch <remote>` + `git rebase <mainline>` on whatever
# branch is checked out (a clean working tree is still required). This suits
# repos where you sit on a feature branch and periodically rebase it onto
# mainline, rather than pulling the configured branch from origin.
#
# The rebase leaves the feature branch's own upstream (@{u}) untouched, so
# `git push` still goes to the feature branch — gar never pushes.
#
# A catch-up repo whose mainline can't be resolved (no explicit value and no
# remote default) is flagged (WARN) rather than silently skipped. Catch-up
# findings are advisory (WARN) — they never fail the run.
#
# Machine-local overlays live in ~/.config/gar/d/ and can add platform- or
# role-scoped catch-up lists (e.g. mac.machine-env-catchup).
#
# Example:
# my-feature-repo|origin/main
